<onlyinclude>'''Dispersed knowledge''' describes knowledge distributed among different people or institutions rather than held in full by any one decision-maker. People often have local knowledge of conditions, needs and opportunities that others do not possess.

== Explanation ==
A warehouse worker may know which item is damaged, while a planner knows demand and a carrier knows travel conditions. Coordinating these observations can improve decisions. Friedrich Hayek used dispersed knowledge to explain a central economic coordination problem and the role that prices may play.

